==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Latinized translation of {{etyl|fr}} {{term|Bohème|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|LL.}} {{term|Boiohæmum|lang=la}}, compound of Germanic -{{recons|haimaz}} ‘home’ (more at {{l|en|home}}) and ''[[w:Boii|Boio]]''- ‘the Boii’, the Celtic tribe previously inhabiting the area. Bohemia was abandoned by the Boii ''ca''. 60 {{smallcaps|b}}.{{smallcaps|c}}. and settled by the Germanic [[w:Marcomanni|Marcomanni]] shortly thereafter.<ref>Dietz, Karlheinz (Würzburg). “Boiohaemum.” ''Brill’s New Pauly'', 2012. Reference. 14 March 2012 <http://referenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/brill-s-new-pauly/boiohaemum-e218860></ref> Related to {{l|en|Bavaria}}.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# a [[region]] in the west of the former [[Czechoslovakia]] and present-day [[Czech Republic]].

